 Version Control for Grafana Dashboards
Objectives
Design and operate a Git-backed dashboard lifecycle system that exports, versions, and restores Grafana dashboards through a scriptable API layer
Implement a multi-branch workflow that isolates experimental dashboard changes from production state and supports tagged releases
Build an automated backup and diff pipeline that compares dashboard JSON across Git refs and restores any historical version to a live Grafana instance
Prerequisites
Comfort with Linux shell scripting, file permissions, and process management on Ubuntu
Familiarity with Git branching, tagging, and the git checkout <ref> -- <file> restore pattern
Understanding of REST API concepts including HTTP verbs, authentication headers, and JSON response parsing
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Provision the Environment
Step 1.1: Install Runtime Dependencies
Your system requires Git, Docker, curl, and jq (a command-line JSON processor). Install them from the Ubuntu package index, then start the Docker daemon and grant your user socket access so subsequent docker commands run without sudo.

sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y git docker.io curl jq tree

sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
newgrp docker
Confirm success by running each binary with its version flag. All four must print a version string with exit code 0.

git --version && docker --version && curl --version | head -1 && jq --version
Troubleshoot this step:

You may see E: Package 'docker.io' has no installation candidate if the package index is stale.
Re-run sudo apt-get update -y and retry; if the package name has changed, consult the official Docker Engine install guide.
Official guide: https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/
Step 1.2: Deploy Grafana and Initialise the Git Repository
Deploy Grafana as a Docker container with a host-mounted volume for persistence. Grafana (an open-source metrics visualisation platform) stores dashboard state in its internal SQLite database inside /var/lib/grafana; mounting that path to the host ensures data survives container restarts.

The directory that Docker mounts must be owned by UID 472, which is the non-root user Grafana runs as inside the container.

mkdir -p "$HOME/grafana-vc/data" "$HOME/grafana-vc/repo"
sudo chown -R 472:472 "$HOME/grafana-vc/data"

docker run -d \
  --name grafana \
  -p 3000:3000 \
  -v "$HOME/grafana-vc/data:/var/lib/grafana" \
  -e GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=LabPass99 \
  grafana/grafana:latest
Official Grafana Docker image reference: https://hub.docker.com/r/grafana/grafana

Wait for the HTTP health endpoint to return "ok" before proceeding.

until curl -fsSL http://localhost:3000/api/health | jq -e '.database == "ok"' > /dev/null 2>&1; do
  echo "Waiting for Grafana..."; sleep 5
done
echo "Grafana is ready"
Troubleshoot this step:

The loop may hang indefinitely if the container exited immediately after start, producing no HTTP listener on port 3000.
Run docker logs grafana to inspect startup errors; a common cause is the data directory having wrong ownership â verify with ls -ln "$HOME/grafana-vc/data" and confirm the owner UID is 472.
Official guide: https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/installation/docker/
